<BlogPost
  title="Dairy Queen Gluten-Free & Allergen Guide (2026)"
  description="What to order at Dairy Queen with a gluten, dairy, peanut or tree nut allergy, which Blizzards to avoid, and how to lower cross-contact risk."
  h1="Dairy Queen Gluten-Free and Allergen Guide"
  crumb="DQ Allergen Guide"
  eyebrow="Allergies and dietary needs"
  image="/Images/non-dairy-dilly-bar.webp"
  imageAlt="Dairy Queen Non-Dairy Dilly Bar"
  datePublished="2026-05-05"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <div role="note" style="background:#FEF2F2;border:1px solid #FECACA;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:20px;color:#7F1D1D;font-size:.95rem;">
    <strong>Important:</strong> This is general information from an independent site, not medical advice. Dairy Queen restaurants use shared equipment, and recipes change. If you have a serious allergy or celiac disease, check Dairy Queen’s official allergen guide and speak with the store before ordering.
  </div>

  <p class="lead"><strong>Dairy Queen can work for some food allergies, but nothing made in the restaurant is guaranteed allergen-free.</strong> Blizzards are blended on shared mixers, fries often share oil with breaded chicken, and cone dip can pick up crumbs. Pre-packaged treats and simple orders, like soft serve in a cup, carry less risk than Blizzards or fried food.</p>

  <h2 id="cross-contact">Where cross-contact happens at DQ</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Blizzard mixers.</strong> The same spindle blends Oreo, Reese’s and pecan Blizzards. You can ask staff to clean it first, but residue is still possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Fryers.</strong> At many stores, fries, cheese curds and breaded chicken share oil.</li>
    <li><strong>Cone dip.</strong> Dipped cones go into a shared dip, which can collect cone crumbs.</li>
    <li><strong>Toppings.</strong> Sundae toppings and Blizzard mix-ins are scooped from open containers near each other.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="lower-risk">Lower-risk choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wrapped novelties</strong> such as the <a href="/items/dilly-bar">Dilly Bar</a> and <a href="/items/non-dairy-dilly-bar">Non-Dairy Dilly Bar</a> are not handled at the Blizzard station. Some stores make Dilly Bars on site, so ask, and check DQ’s guide for allergens.</li>
    <li><strong>Soft serve in a cup</strong> avoids the cone and the Blizzard mixer.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/items/misty-slush">Misty Slush</a></strong> is made without dairy in most flavors.</li>
    <li><strong>Simple sundaes</strong> like a <a href="/items/strawberry-sundae">strawberry sundae</a> skip the mixer, but toppings are scooped near others.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="blizzards">Blizzard allergens at a glance</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="size-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Blizzard flavors</th><th>Wheat / gluten</th><th>Peanuts / tree nuts</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{blizzards.map((b) => <tr><td>{b.name}</td><td>{b.wheat}</td><td>{b.nuts}</td><td>{b.note}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size:.88rem;color:var(--color-text-muted);">All Blizzards contain milk. "Check" means confirm with DQ’s allergen guide; ingredients can change.</p>

  <h2 id="gluten">Gluten-free at Dairy Queen</h2>
  <p>Plain soft serve does not have gluten ingredients, but cones, cookie and brownie mix-ins, Texas toast, buns and breaded chicken all do. Because of shared fryers and mixers, DQ is not a safe choice for celiac disease without talking to the store. If you are avoiding gluten for a milder reason, a sundae or soft serve cup is the simplest order.</p>

  <h2 id="dairy-free">Dairy-free at Dairy Queen</h2>
  <p>Almost every DQ treat contains milk. The main dairy-free options are the <a href="/items/non-dairy-dilly-bar">Non-Dairy Dilly Bar</a> and most <a href="/items/misty-slush">Misty Slush</a> flavors. On the food side, check the bun, cheese and sauces on any burger.</p>

  <h2 id="nuts">Peanut and tree nut allergies</h2>
  <p>Peanut and pecan Blizzards, the <a href="/items/buster-bar">Buster Bar</a> and the <a href="/items/peanut-buster-parfait">Peanut Buster Parfait</a> contain nuts. Because the Blizzard mixer is shared, people with severe nut allergies should skip Blizzards.</p>

  <h2 id="how-to-ask">How to order with an allergy</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Tell the cashier about the allergy before you order, not after.</li>
    <li>Ask them to change gloves and clean the mixer or scoop.</li>
    <li>Order during a quiet time, when staff can take extra care.</li>
    <li>If anything seems unsure, skip it. A wrapped novelty or Misty Slush is usually the lowest-risk choice.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>For calories and lighter options, see our <a href="/blog/dairy-queen-calories-nutrition-guide">DQ calories guide</a>.</p>
</BlogPost>
